- CAPTURE CEILING (PO 2026-08-22 "record another Chrome tab with a 4K game → video freezes") — answers the open surface
  question above (ONE tab) and is a SECOND, different root cause: throughput, not liveness. Frames arrive in bursts rather
  than stopping dead, so the 3s stall detector never fires. A 4K surface is paid for four times on one GPU (Chrome capture
  readback + raw 8 Mbps encode + composite <video> decode & downscale 30×/s + on-screen preview decode) while the captured
  tab renders the game — and every one of those pixels is discarded at export, which is 1080p on both paths. Ship: display
  track constrained to DEFAULT_EXPORT_SETTINGS (CAPTURE_MAX_* in acquire.ts) as MAX-only constraints in the getDisplayMedia
  request + capDisplayTrack() re-apply before the channel is delivered (before its recorder exists; bounded 1.5s, failure
  tolerant). frameRate max 30, not just ideal — a 60fps game tab otherwise doubles the encode for nothing. MEASURED (real
  Chrome, hidden tab, AudioWorklet rig mirroring liveComposite, 2 runs order-reversed, idle machine): 4K delivered 21.5/11.9
  fps vs a 30 target, drawImage 13.94/8.55ms of a 33ms budget, composite encoder starved to 289/106 KB/s — same rig at 1080p
  delivered 29.8–29.9 fps, drawImage 0.97–1.04ms, encoders 597–614 KB/s. Also: per-source delivered fps logged every 10s
  from the composite tick (console only — a static screen legitimately delivers ~1 keep-alive fps). STILL NEEDS: PO recheck
